ES|QL: lazy COALESCE filters every column of the page once per null position

When a `COALESCE` child isn't safe to evaluate eagerly (`eagerEvalSafeInLazy() == false`, e.g. a `CASE` or a function that can warn), `Coalesce$Type$LazyEvaluator` handles each position whose earlier result is null on its own: it filters **every block of the page** down to that one position and evaluates the remaining children on that one-row page ([`X-CoalesceEvaluator.java.st` L218-L262](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/f724085bf7803aaaa1319d1ae8eef0459cb52f20/x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/expression/function/scalar/nulls/X-CoalesceEvaluator.java.st#L218-L262)):

```
position: for (int p = 0; p < positionCount; p++) {
    if (lastFullBlock.isNull(p) == false) { ... continue; }
    int[] positions = new int[] { p };
    Page limited = new Page(1, IntStream.range(0, page.getBlockCount())
        .mapToObj(b -> page.getBlock(b).filter(false, positions)) ...);
    for (int e = firstToEvaluate; e < evaluators.size(); e++) {
        ... evaluators.get(e).eval(limited) ...
    }
}
```

That's one filter of every column, plus one evaluator call per child, for every null position: O(null positions × columns). A page as wide as it is after a large `STATS` (dozens of columns, some multivalued) pays for all of them, though the children read one or two.

For example, cleaning several fallback fields after a `STATS` takes this path for every row whose first choice is missing:

```
| EVAL browser = COALESCE(
    CASE(TRIM(raw_browser_name) IN ("", "null"), null, TRIM(raw_browser_name)),
    CASE(TRIM(raw_user_agent_name) IN ("", "null"), null, TRIM(raw_user_agent_name)))
```

- Do what `CaseLazyEvaluator` does per arm: collect the positions that are still null, filter the page once to those, evaluate the next child on that page, scatter its non-null values back, and repeat with the positions that are still null.
- Filter only the channels the child reads. `Case.java` has the same TODO for its lazy arms ([L731](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/f724085bf7803aaaa1319d1ae8eef0459cb52f20/x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/expression/function/scalar/conditional/Case.java#L731), [L860](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/f724085bf7803aaaa1319d1ae8eef0459cb52f20/x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/expression/function/scalar/conditional/Case.java#L860)): "filtering every block copies columns the arm never looks at".

Found while profiling the Kibana Digital Experience session summary query ([kibana#306078](https://github.com/elastic/kibana/pull/306078)), which runs nine such `COALESCE`s on the coordinator over about 4,000 rows and 75 columns. There it costs about 20 ms, and rewriting it to take the eager path made no measurable difference to the whole query. It matters more for wide pages with many null positions, or for the same pattern on data nodes before a `STATS`.

ES|QL: CASE(condition, value, null) copies the value one position at a time

`CaseEagerEvaluator` evaluates both arms on the whole page, then builds the result one position at a time ([`Case.java` L950-L961](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/f724085bf7803aaaa1319d1ae8eef0459cb52f20/x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/expression/function/scalar/conditional/Case.java#L950-L961)):

```
for (int p = 0; p < lhs.getPositionCount(); p++) {
    if (lhsOrRhs.mask().getBoolean(p)) {
        // TODO Copy the per-type specialization that COALESCE has.
        builder.copyFrom(lhs, p, p + 1);
    } else {
        builder.copyFrom(rhs, p, p + 1);
    }
}
```

When the else is the `null` literal, the result is just the value block with the non-matching positions set to null, which is what `Block#keepMask(BooleanVector)` already does in bulk. The mask from `toMask()` already treats null and multivalued conditions as false.

`CASE(condition, value, null)` is the usual way to make a value conditional before an aggregation, so it tends to run on every row:

```
| EVAL seen_user = CASE(attributes.user.id IS NOT NULL OR attributes.user.name IS NOT NULL, @timestamp, null),
       lcp = CASE(vital == "lcp", vital_value, null)
| STATS user = LAST(attributes.user.id, seen_user), lcp_p75 = PERCENTILE(lcp, 75) BY session
```

When the else value is the `null` literal, return `lhs.keepMask(lhsOrRhs.mask())` instead of the copy loop. The per-type specialization in the TODO would still help the general case.

The Kibana Digital Experience session summary query ([kibana#306078](https://github.com/elastic/kibana/pull/306078)) evaluates four of these on every event before its `STATS`, and the data node `EvalOperator` was about 55% of its profile. I haven't measured a patched build.

IndexShard#updateShardState: stale shard-started after master failover may not be replica-only

`IndexShard#updateShardState` has an assert whose justification doesn't appear to actually hold. Found while investigating a reviewer comment on an unrelated PR (https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/pull/165766#discussion_r4612335439), not from an observed production incident or test failure.

[`7ee9adcfda4a` "Fail shard if STARTED after master failover" (#87451)](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/commit/7ee9adcfda4ae0d817b2ddd9f474ea17c250fbd0), closing #87367, added this handling in [`IndexShard#updateShardState`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/9ec5c89d8ba22217990e4e485591b74dfead5981/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/index/shard/IndexShard.java#L661-L671):

```
if (newRouting.active() && state != IndexShardState.STARTED && state != IndexShardState.CLOSED) {
    // If cluster.no_master_block: all then we remove all shards locally whenever there's no master, but there might still be
    // a shard-started message in flight. When the new master is elected we start to recover our shards again and the stale
    // shard-started message could arrive and move this shard to STARTED in the cluster state too soon. This is pretty rare so
    // we fix it by just failing the shard and starting the recovery again.
    //
    // NB this can only happen on replicas - if it happened to a primary then we'd move to a new primary term and ignore the
    // stale shard-started message.
    assert newRouting.primary() == false
        : "primary routing is active, but local shard state isn't. routing: " + newRouting + ", local state: " + state;
[... 2 more lines]
```

The scenario it's guarding: with `cluster.no_master_block: all`, a node that loses its view of the master wipes its local shards (`IndicesClusterStateService`'s `disableStatePersistence` branch). A `shardStarted` message already in flight for a just-completed recovery survives this (`ShardStateAction`'s `waitForNewMasterAndRetry` keeps retrying it across master changes, independent of anything happening locally). Once a new master is elected, the node starts a fresh recovery for the same allocation id. If the stale, retried `shardStarted` then lands on the new master before the new recovery finishes, master marks the shard `STARTED` while the local shard is still mid-recovery. The `throw` handles this correctly (fails the shard, recovery restarts) for *any* shard type — the `assert` is only claiming this specific interleaving can't happen for primaries, because a primary would supposedly get a new primary term in the process, which would make `ShardStartedTaskExecutor` reject the stale message instead (primary-term mismatch check).

Primary term only bumps via `IndexMetadataUpdater#shardInitialized` (new allocation, i.e. master actually reallocated this shard) or `IndexMetadataUpdater#shardFailed` (an already-`active()` primary failing) — see the class's own doc comment and both methods:
https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/9ec5c89d8ba22217990e4e485591b74dfead5981/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/cluster/routing/allocation/IndexMetadataUpdater.java#L40-L98

Neither fires just because a node locally decided "no master" and wiped its own state. Tracing the actual join/election path:

- [`NodeJoinExecutor#execute`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/9ec5c89d8ba22217990e4e485591b74dfead5981/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/cluster/coordination/NodeJoinExecutor.java#L120-L148): `disassociateDeadNodes` is only invoked from the one-time "become master" transition (`becomeMasterAndTrimConflictingNodes`, line 132). A join processed *after* a master is already established goes through the `else if (currentNodes.isLocalNodeElectedMaster())` branch, which does nothing to routing at all — an already-known node rejoining just logs and returns.
- [`becomeMasterAndTrimConflictingNodes`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/9ec5c89d8ba22217990e4e485591b74dfead5981/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/cluster/coordination/NodeJoinExecutor.java#L318-L341): only removes a previously-known node from `nodesBuilder` if it *conflicts* with an incoming joiner (same node id but a different node object, or same address but a different id — e.g. a restarted node reusing an address/port). A node that isn't part of this specific join batch and doesn't conflict with anything is carried forward into `tmpState.nodes()` untouched.
- [`AllocationService#disassociateDeadNodes`](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/9ec5c89d8ba22217990e4e485591b74dfead5981/server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/cluster/routing/allocation/AllocationService.java#L326-L340): "clear from the shards any node id they used to belong to that is now dead" — only fails shards allocated to nodes *absent* from `clusterState.nodes()`.

So whether the primary term bumps in this scenario depends entirely on whether some *independent* fault-detection mechanism (follower/leader checks, `NodesFaultDetection`, an explicit shard-failure report, etc.) separately decided this specific node was gone during the gap. That's decoupled from the node's own `cluster.no_master_block: all` reaction: a node can locally conclude "I have no master" (triggering the local wipe) well before, or entirely without, the rest of the cluster ever suspecting it's unreachable — e.g. during a clean/fast master handoff where this particular data node was never itself flagged as unreachable by anyone. In that case it simply rejoins the new master, is already present in `nodes()` (never removed, never conflicting), its shard allocation and primary term are completely untouched, and the stale retried `shardStarted` can land and mark it `STARTED` while the fresh local recovery attempt is still mid-flight — including for a primary.

- Confirm (ideally with a repro, e.g. via an internal-cluster test driving a master-side join batch directly) whether this interleaving is actually reachable for a primary.
- If so, decide what to do about the `assert newRouting.primary() == false` — the `throw` itself already handles primaries and replicas identically and correctly (fail + restart recovery), so the fix is likely just narrowing/removing the assert and its comment rather than changing behavior.

No customer impact is known; this is a code-reading investigation, not an observed incident.

